  "question": {
    "text": "Consider the following reaction, the rate expression of which is given below\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C} \\\\\n& \\text { rate }=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{1 / 2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{1 / 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe reaction is initiated by taking \\(1 \\mathrm{M}\\) concentration of \\(\\mathrm{A}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{B}\\) each. If the rate constant \\((\\mathrm{k})\\) is \\(4.6 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}\\), then the time taken for \\(\\mathrm{A}\\) to become \\(0.1 \\mathrm{M}\\) is ______ sec. (nearest integer)",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "50",
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{K}=\\frac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log \\frac{1}{0.1} \\\\ & 4.6 \\times 10^{-2}=\\frac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}=50 \\mathrm{sec} .\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
